{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Refined Prototype and Capstone Defense","title":"Evidence-of-Iteration Documentation","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting evidence-of-iteration documentation to storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next."],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_refined_prototype_and_capstone_defense","ail606_supplement_evidence_of_iteration_documentation","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":1,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"06","section":"01","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/05/08"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_6_1_evidence_of_iteration_documentation_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_6_1_evidence_of_iteration_documentation_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_6_1_evidence_of_iteration_documentation_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_6_1_evidence_of_iteration_documentation_calibration"]},"content":"# Evidence-of-Iteration Documentation\n\n![Evidence-of-Iteration Documentation textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/06-01-evidence-of-iteration-documentation.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA capstone committee almost never disputes that your prototype changed between versions. What they probe is whether the change was driven by evidence or by taste. Evidence-of-iteration documentation is the genre of writing that answers that probe in advance. It is the difference between a portfolio that shows three screenshots of \"v1, v2, v3\" and a portfolio that shows, for each version, the specific observation that triggered the revision, the design principle that interpreted that observation, and the alternative you considered and declined. In design research this is the distinction between a product log and a design rationale: the product log records what the artifact looks like, while the rationale records why it looks that way and why it does not look the other way.\n\nThis matters because instructional design is a defensible-decision profession, not a deliverable-production profession. Your storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes is the deliverable; the iteration record is the evidence that the deliverable is the result of reasoning a peer could reproduce.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA student submits a four-frame onboarding storyboard with a one-line changelog: \"Tightened the visuals in v2.\" The committee asks a single question: \"What told you the visuals needed tightening?\" The student has no answer, because the change came from a vague sense that the screen felt crowded. The screen may genuinely be better, but the student cannot demonstrate that it is better, and in a defense, an undemonstrable improvement reads as luck.\n\nContrast that with a student who can point to a tryout note: three of three testers skipped the lower half of Frame 3. Now \"tightening\" has a cause, and the revision becomes inspectable. This section is about producing the second kind of record by default, so the design logic behind your storyboard is never hidden inside your own head.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nEvidence-of-iteration documentation is a structured **design rationale**: a record that makes each revision traceable from a triggering observation to a justified change. A defensible iteration entry contains five linked elements, and the linkage is what carries the meaning. Drop any one element and the entry collapses back into a changelog.\n\n| Element | Question it answers | Failure mode if missing |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Triggering observation | What did you actually see a learner do? | \"Felt crowded\" (unfalsifiable intuition) |\n| Interpretive principle | Which theory explains why that is a problem? | Change with no diagnosis (treats symptom, not cause) |\n| Change made | What concretely differs in the artifact? | Vague \"improved\" with no inspectable delta |\n| Result | Did the change move the observed behavior? | Unverified change (no evidence it worked) |\n| Rejected alternative | What did you consider and decline, and why? | Hides that you reasoned about trade-offs |\n\nThe five elements only carry meaning in order: an observation comes before its interpreting principle, the change follows the diagnosis, the result tests the change, and the rejected alternative defends the path taken. Reconstruct that chain so the linkage is explicit.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Triggering observation: 3 of 3 testers skipped the lower half of Frame 3\", \"Interpretive principle: split-attention names why the skip happened\", \"Change made: split Frame 3 into 3a (diagram + short narration) and 3b (prompt)\", \"Result: in re-test, 3 of 3 completed both subframes\", \"Rejected alternative: shortening narration alone, declined because the pause point was the lever\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\nTwo theory anchors do the interpretive work in this genre. Mayer's multimedia principles tell you whether an observation reflects a real learning demand (for example, learners not encoding a step because the diagram and narration competed for attention). Cognitive load theory names the mechanism (split attention, redundancy, transient information) so the revision targets the cause rather than the surface. The discipline rule is strict: if a cited theory did not change a wording, a layout, a timing, or a sequence, it is decoration, not rationale. The \"rejected alternative\" line is the single most diagnostic field, because it is the only one that proves you weighed options instead of accepting the first idea.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nHere is one row from a real iteration log for a four-frame onboarding storyboard. Read the before version, then the annotated after version.\n\n**Before (weak log entry):**\n\n> Frame 3: cleaned it up in v2, looks much better now.\n\nThat entry cannot be reviewed. It names no problem, no source, and no rejected option. Now the same change, documented as evidence of iteration:\n\n**After (annotated decision):**\n\n1. **Problem observed:** In a 3-learner tryout, all 3 skipped the second half of Frame 3. The frame held 84 words of narration plus a diagram with no pause point.\n2. **Source:** Cognitive load theory (split-attention and segmenting). The learner had to hold narration and diagram in working memory at once.\n3. **Change made:** Split Frame 3 into 3a (diagram + 38-word narration) and 3b (28-word narration + a \"predict the next step\" reader-action prompt).\n4. **Result:** In the re-test, 3 of 3 learners completed both subframes and 2 attempted the prompt.\n5. **Rejected alternative:** I considered only shortening the narration to 50 words in one frame. I rejected it because the segmenting evidence said the pause point, not just word count, was the lever.\n\nThe after version is not longer for its own sake. Each numbered line is a thing a reviewer can audit: a problem, a source, a change, a result, and a path not taken.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Audit the Frame 3 entry one element at a time\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The entry opens with \\\"all 3 skipped the second half of Frame 3.\\\" Which element is this, and is it auditable?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"A feeling about the screen\",\"A triggering observation a peer can verify\",\"A rejected alternative\",\"The interpretive principle\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"It is the triggering observation: a quoted learner behavior, not an intuition. A skeptical peer could re-run the tryout and check it.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The entry names split-attention and segmenting. What work is that line doing?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Decorating the entry with a citation\",\"Naming the mechanism so the fix targets the cause, not the symptom\",\"Proving the change worked\",\"Listing a path not taken\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"It is the interpretive principle. It diagnoses why the skip happened (narration and diagram competed in working memory), so the change can target the cause.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The final line says shortening narration to 50 words was considered and declined. Why is this the most diagnostic line?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"It makes the entry longer\",\"It is the only line proving options were weighed instead of accepting the first idea\",\"It restates the observation\",\"It polishes the prose\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"The rejected alternative is the single field that proves you reasoned about trade-offs. Drop it and the entry cannot show you chose among options.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nWhen you finish an iteration entry, run it through a falsifiability test before you trust it: could a skeptical peer disagree with this entry using your own evidence? If the entry only contains praise of the new version, there is nothing to disagree with, which means there is nothing to verify.\n\n1. **Observation test:** Does the entry quote a learner behavior or a measured value, not a feeling about the screen?\n2. **Mechanism test:** Does a named principle explain why that behavior is a problem, so the fix targets the cause?\n3. **Counterfactual test:** Is there a rejected alternative that shows you chose among options rather than accepting the first?\n\nIf any test fails, the artifact may be finished, but the iteration record is not yet evidence. Add the missing line before you move on.\n\n<inline-check question=\"An entry reads: \\\"Learners hesitated on Frame 2, so I redesigned it and it tests much better now.\\\" Which falsifiability test does this entry fail hardest?\" explanation=\"It quotes a behavior (hesitation) but never names a principle, so the change has no diagnosis. The mechanism test fails: nothing explains why the hesitation is a problem, so the fix cannot be shown to target the cause.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The observation test, because no learner behavior is named\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The mechanism test, because no named principle explains why the behavior is a problem\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"No test, the entry is already evidence\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The counterfactual test only, because the result is missing\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common error is treating iteration documentation as a writing-up step performed after the design is done. Reconstructed rationale is weaker than recorded rationale, because memory smooths over the rejected alternatives that make the record honest. Capture the triggering observation in the moment you see it, even as a one-line annotation, and write the full entry while the tryout is fresh.\n\nA second misreading is to let an AI assistant polish the prose of the storyboard while the design logic stays undocumented. A more fluent caption is not a justified caption. Keep the tool on the surface layer and keep the rationale in your own observations.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nTrace one revision end to end on your own storyboard. Pick a single frame and ask what a learner is supposed to do on it, not what it is supposed to show. Run a small tryout, even with two or three readers, and write down the first place a reader hesitates, backtracks, or skips. That hesitation is your triggering observation.\n\nNow reach for one principle, not three. If the reader skipped narration while studying a diagram, the split-attention principle is the relevant lens; reach for it and let it dictate the fix (integrate the label into the diagram, or segment the narration). Make the change, re-test the same frame, and record whether the hesitation disappeared. Finally, write the alternative you did not choose. The walkthrough is complete only when someone who never watched your tryout could read the entry and agree the change was warranted.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read this section, keep your own iteration log open and tag each design move you have already made with the five elements above. The diagnostic question is not \"is my storyboard good\" but \"could I defend this specific frame against a committee member who liked the previous version.\" Where you cannot, you have found the frame whose rationale is still missing.\n\nA strong reader of evidence-of-iteration documentation can do four things:\n\n1. State the triggering observation for any given revision without re-watching the tryout.\n2. Name the principle that turned that observation into a diagnosis.\n3. Produce the rejected alternative on request.\n4. Explain what they would log differently if they ran the tryout again.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong iteration record is judged by reproducibility, not by length. A peer should be able to read your entry, look at the two versions, and arrive at the same change you made. That standard fails the moment an entry asserts an improvement the evidence does not show, so treat every \"better\" as a claim that needs an observation attached. If a frame has visible polish but no triggering observation, mark it with an annotation and request a targeted before/after comparison rather than a general critique.\n\nA final paragraph for the artifact would read like this: \"I changed the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes because a tryout showed learners skipping content I assumed they were reading, and a named principle told me why. I declined a competing fix because it would have changed the surface without addressing the cause the evidence identified.\"\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"In your own words, explain why recorded rationale (captured in the moment) is stronger evidence than reconstructed rationale (written from memory after the design is done). Tie your answer to the rejected-alternative element specifically.\" answer=\"Recorded rationale captures the triggering observation and the alternatives you actually weighed while they were still in front of you. Memory smooths over the paths not taken, so a reconstructed entry tends to keep only the change that worked and quietly drops the rejected alternative, which is the single most diagnostic field. Without that field the entry cannot show you chose among options, so it collapses back toward a changelog even if the surrounding prose is fluent.\"></self-explain>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_evidence_of_iteration_documentation\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\", \"ail606_supplement_refined_prototype_and_capstone_defense\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same storyboard into a setting with no tryout learners available, say a tight enterprise timeline where you cannot recruit testers before the deadline. The five-element structure does not disappear; the triggering observation simply changes source. Instead of an observed skip, you might rely on a documented heuristic violation or an analytics pattern from a comparable module. The test of whether you understand iteration documentation is whether you can keep the rationale honest when direct observation is unavailable, substituting the best evidence you can get rather than dropping the evidence requirement entirely.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/06-01-evidence-of-iteration-documentation-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"06.01\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **Mayer multimedia learning**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes for AIL 606 06.01. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to cognitive load theory. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_evidence_of_iteration_documentation\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure each iteration entry links a triggering observation, an interpretive principle, the concrete change, the verified result, and a rejected alternative. That five-part chain is what turns a changelog into evidence of design judgment.\n\n## References\n\n- Mayer multimedia learning. https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_6_1_evidence_of_iteration_documentation_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_evidence_of_iteration_documentation","stem":"A designer's iteration log for a branching storyboard reads: 'v1, v2, v3, v4, v5 - kept refining the screens.' What is the strongest objection a capstone reviewer would raise?","options":["Five versions is too few to count as real iteration.","The log proves activity but not learning: it never names the problem each version was responding to.","Version numbers should use semantic versioning such as 1.0.1.","The designer should have stopped at v3 to save time."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"An iteration log earns credit when each version is tied to a problem-change-result chain, not by the count of versions.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_6_1_version_count_as_iteration","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_evidence_of_iteration_documentation"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_6_1_evidence_of_iteration_documentation_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"Two log entries describe the SAME storyboard frame. Which one documents a design iteration rather than a polish change?","options":["Changed the heading font from Arial to Calibri and recentered the title.","Recolored the Next button from gray to blue for brand consistency.","Split one dense narration frame into two and added a reader-action prompt between them after testers skipped the segment.","Adjusted the slide margins from 0.5 in to 0.75 in."],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"A design diff changes what the learner must do or process; the other three are styling-only polish diffs.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_6_1_polish_diff_as_design_diff","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_evidence_of_iteration_documentation"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_6_1_evidence_of_iteration_documentation_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"You ask an AI assistant to help write the rationale column of your iteration log. Which use keeps the documentation defensible?","options":["Have the AI invent plausible-sounding rationales for every change so the log looks complete.","Paste your tryout notes and rubric lines, then ask the AI to draft rationale wording you verify against those sources before keeping it.","Let the AI decide which changes were important and delete the rest from the log.","Ask the AI to rate your storyboard so you can list its score as the evidence."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"AI may help phrase a rationale grounded in real sources you supply and verify; it must not manufacture the evidence itself.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_6_1_unsourced_change_rationale","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_6_1_evidence_of_iteration_documentation_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"A reviewer says your iteration log 'shows decisions but not judgment.' Which single addition best answers that critique?","options":["A larger thumbnail of each final screen.","A timestamp on every edit.","For at least one change, the alternative design you considered and rejected, with the reason it lost.","A word count for each narration block."],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Keeping a rejected alternative with its reason exposes the trade-off you weighed, which is what makes judgment auditable.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_6_1_iteration_log_hides_rejected_paths","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_evidence_based_revision"]}]}}